.TH MACHO-EXPLORER 1 "October 4, 2026" "MachO-explorer 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
macho-explorer \- Mach\-O binary introspection
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B macho-explorer
.B audit
.RI [ binary | bundle ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.BR [ \-\-sarif ]
.RB [ \-\-strict ]
.RB [ \-\-arch= name ]
.RB [ \-b | \-\-binary
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Checks every structural claim the file makes about itself and reports a verdict,
with an exit status a build can gate on.
.BR describe (1)
already reports abnormalities per slice; this exists because a report is not a
gate.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
sound \- no findings at the requested strictness
.TP
.B 1
unsound \- at least one finding. A
.IR "negative answer" ,
not an error
.TP
.B 2
usage error
.TP
.B 3
could not read the file at all
.PP
1 rather than 3 is the important distinction, and it follows the taxonomy every
tool here uses: exit 1 means the tool ran and its answer was no. An audit that found
something wrong has done its job. The distinction from 3 matters in the other
direction too, because an unreadable file is
.I not
a passing audit:
.B audit /nope
exits 3, so a mistyped path in a CI script cannot be read as a clean bill of health.
.SH SEVERITY
Findings carry a severity, on one question: can the reader's answers still be
trusted?
.TP
.B error
the file disagrees with itself \- a size or an extent points at bytes that are not
there, so anything computed from it may be wrong
.TP
.B warning
the file parsed and the answers are probably right, but something is unfamiliar or
explicitly heuristic
.PP
By default only errors fail.
.B \-\-strict
fails on warnings too, which is right for a release gate you control the toolchain
for and wrong otherwise: a binary built by an Xcode newer than this reader sets a
flag bit the header has no name for, and failing every build over that trains people
to disable the gate.
.SH WHAT IS NOT A FINDING
Unknown
.I load commands
are not. They are already named by number by
.BR "describe \-\-loads" ,
on the principle that an unfamiliar-but-valid command is present rather than broken;
grading it as damage here would fire on every binary from a newer linker.
.PP
An
.B LC_MAIN
entry point outside
.B __TEXT
is not either. That is the normal state of a dyld shared-cache stub, and measurement
found it on a fully-symbolled 113 MB
.I node
as well. A warning that is always true teaches a reader to skip warnings.
.SH THE FAT TABLE
Overlapping slices
.I are
a finding, and the reason is worth stating: both slices parse cleanly and no
per-slice check can see it, because each is internally consistent. What it costs is
visible in the
.I bent
fixture, where the second slice's bytes overwrite the first's string table and that
slice goes on to report six symbol entries and no names \- a shape otherwise
indistinguishable from a stripped binary.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.nf
macho-explorer audit build/Contents/MacOS/app
macho-explorer audit \-\-strict ./dist/*.dylib
macho-explorer audit \-\-json ./out | jq '.data.clean, .data.counts'
macho-explorer audit \-\-sarif ./out > audit.sarif
.fi
.SH SARIF
.B \-\-sarif
writes a SARIF 2.1.0 document to stdout and a one\-line summary to stderr, for GitHub
Code Scanning and any other SARIF consumer. Two properties are deliberate:
.PP
Each finding is a rule named by the
.I kind
the reader already reports, so a second run matches the first run's findings rather
than filing a new alert on every build. And an unreadable file yields a valid
document with
.I no
findings \- a mistyped path in a workflow is a bug in the workflow, not damage to
the binary, and reporting it as a finding would put an annotation on the commit for
something the commit did not do.
.PP
.B \-\-sarif
and
.B \-\-json
are two formats for one answer and cannot be combined; doing so is a usage error
rather than a precedence rule, because silently preferring one produces a
.I .sarif
file containing JSON that fails to parse much later with an error naming neither
flag.
.PP
Use
.I data.clean
or
.I data.strictClean
as the gate, not
.IR data.verdict ,
which is a label for a person:
.I verdict: "warnings"
with
.I clean: true
is a
.I passing
audit.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR describe (1),
.BR fingerprint (1),
.BR diff (1),
.BR sym (1)